  "result": "I've written the plan but haven't changed any code, since plan mode is on. I couldn't call ExitPlanMode because the tool isn't available here, so this message is the approval request.\n\n**Cause:** `Context.__init__` in `src/click/core.py` (around lines 385-394) hands the parent's whole `default_map` to the child context. With `default_map={'sub': {'opt': 1}}`, the subcommand's context sees the key `sub` instead of `opt`, so its lookup misses. A subcommand option that happens to be named `sub` would also wrongly receive the dict. The docs already describe the nested form, so the code contradicts them.\n\n**Fix:** In that `__init__`, use `parent.default_map.get(info_name)` so the child gets only its own sub-dict. Guard against a non-mapping parent map. Treat an `UNSET` value as no entry. `Group.invoke`, `Command.make_context` and `Context.invoke` all create the child through this constructor, so none of them need changes.\n\n**Tests:** I'd add cases to `tests/test_defaults.py`:\n- A group default reaching a subcommand option, with its source reported as `DEFAULT_MAP`.\n- A two-level nested map.\n- An option named like its subcommand not receiving the dict.\n- A missing subcommand key falling back to normal defaults.\n- `ctx.invoke` using the nested map.\n\n**Changelog:** Add a bullet to the top section of `CHANGES.md`. I'll check whether version 8.5.0 is still unreleased first.\n\n**Verification:** Run `pytest` on `tests/test_defaults.py`, `tests/test_context.py` and `tests/test_options.py`, then the full suite.\n\nTell me to proceed and I'll make these changes.",
